  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - Central idea: We use a hierarchical Bayesian optimization framework that links solutions across different grid resolutions (45, 90, 180, 360 points) using interpolated solutions as initial points.\n   - Why different: The original COBYLA approach gets stuck in shallow local optima. We introduce gradient-based Bayesian optimization (using Treed Gaussian Process) with automatic differentiability and multi-resolution refinement.\n   - Bottleneck: The correlation operation remains expensive; proposed solution includes caching gradient estimates and adaptive lookahead selection.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Representation: Piecewise constant function on intervals, represented by n-point grid samples.\n   - Objective: minimize C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Variables: h-values at grid points, with integral constraint \u222b h(x)dx = 1\n   - Optimization: Treed Gaussian Process as surrogate, with derivative-based exploration\n   - Constraints: \n     - Box: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n     - Equality: \u2211h_i = n/2\n   - Initialization: Multi-resolution interpolation from parent solution\n   - Feasibility: Integral adjustment via scaling and repair mechanisms\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Initialize hierarchical grid (45, 90, 180, 360 points)\n   - Set up Treed GP with adaptive Mat\u00e9rn kernel and pointwise uncertainties\n   - Use BFGS for local refinement and expected improvement heuristic for global search\n   - Cache gradient estimates for faster iterations\n   - Store solutions with score history and detect convergence patterns\n   - Multi-resolution cascade using interpolated parent as seed\n   - Key operations logged incrementally with dimension checks\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Resolution sets: {45, 90, 180, 360}\n   - Optimization method: adaptive stepsize with lookahead gradient check\n   - Initial seeding: Hierarchical interpolation from parent solution\n   - Termination:\n     - Relative improvement < 1e-4\n     - Grid doubling without improvement\n     - Exceeded grid dimension limit\n   - Mode selection: Progressive grid expansion for optimality\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n   - Dominant cost: Greedy lookahead evaluations (\u224820 per grid level)\n   - Schedule: Prioritize low-resolution grids for rapid improvement\n   - Repair: Integral adjustment via scaling, gradient projection\n   - Fallback: Return best found solution within time\n   - Convergence check: Track best score improvements (stall detection)\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Post-processing: Re-evaluate final solution via original metric\n   - Format: Return (h_values, score, n_points)\n   - Safety checks:\n     * Box constraints: Ensure 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\n     * Integral: |\u2211h_i*(2/n) - 1| < 1e-6\n     * Correlation: Verify via finite differencing\n   - Output: h_values.reshape(-1,1) for requested resolution\n   - Error handling: Subgradient regression, fallback seeds",
